You overstate the difficulty, in my experience.

My usual strategy is:

1) Enter Mines with one, preferably more, 9HD pet.
That just means lingering on the main dungeon levels
above level 5, before entering Mines at all beyond
the need run down and right back up the stairs to
identify which ones _are_ the Mines stairs, until
your pets are buffed up.

This requires only patience, no special skill at
all, since the monsters get no more difficult while
I stay XL 1, and requires letting my pets do _all_
the killing, [or, for classes that need at least one
XP to get positive responses to prayers for
sustenance, having exactly one newt killed by my
PC], both because I don't want the XPs and because
they do need them to level up. Every monster I kill
instead of my pet is XP I don't want and an HP per
kill that my pet doesn't receive to improve its HD.

2) Fight from the steps at each level until the
initial batch of monsters created with the level
(usually at least a couple of dozen in my
experience) have been removed by my pets, retreating
up the stairs early and often. [I don't _care_ that
the monsters recover their HPs while I'm gone, I
care that I still have some HPs left when my pets
finally kill off the initial set of monsters.]

3) Explore slowly, cautiously, and completely on
each level. I usually do a "right hand rule" around
the level once I step away from the stairs, though
that only partially works, the levels aren't classic
mazes where that tactic works fully. Getting an
outline of the level is a valuable beginning though,
so I do.

We're in no hurry at all doing this exploration,
because we're not just passing through, we're here
with a _purpose_.

We're busily acquiring goodies, both randomly
generated and death-dropped ones, and we're willing
to stall around as long as needed to let lots of
monsters be randomly generated to increase the
death-drop haul.

The longer you stay on a Mines level, the better
mapped the traps become and the fewer are the nasty
surprises, so the levels grow safer with longer
occupancy. A fine thing to do while waiting is to
use a happily-gotten pick-axe to tunnel between the
cul-de-sacs to improve your pets' ability to
navigate back to you, and to give you loops around
which to run from monsters while trying to attract
the help of your frequently strayed pets.

We really, really are focused on picking up and
trying on every bit of armor pets confirm as safe
right now.

That is our *whole purpose* for entering Mines _at
all_ *now* -- cheap, good AC, early in the game, is
far more easily gotten here than in the main
dungeon.

There is otherwise _nothing_ in Mines _required_
this early in the game otherwise to go on, just some
candles needed much, much later, that can easily be
deferred clear until the VS has been located, if
desired, and a nice-to-have luckstone far too deep
to be worth the risk this early.

This is where our first real survivable AC kit gets
collected, tested, and constructed.

Exploring each Mines level fully is a scary thing,
but doable.

A wizard is really lucky, magic resistence makes the
nastiest, magic based traps dismissable, but also
really unlucky, because some of the piffling
physical based ones can kill you, like spiked pits,
with you probably with at most a dozen HPs as yet.

It helps a _lot_ to have pets running around finding
all the traps before you do (and using them as crash
test dummies is another reason you need them buffed
up before entering Mines, or they just promptly die
in their efforts), so take careful note of their
paths, and step only where they've stepped whenever
possible, you'll live lots longer.

> Enter the mines with non-fighter character classes
> when you have prepared them in the main dungeon,
> not sooner.

Well, I limit that to: explore and exploit all of
DLVL 1-4 before entering Mines.

I'm still trying to go in with 0 to 1 XP if at all
possible. My main-dungeon targets are shops and
vaults and random goodies and gold, not AC
improvements yet. There just isn't enough "stuff" in
the main dungeon top four layers from which to build
a buff wardrobe for a wizard, unless I find an
exceptional armor shop, or a book store full of
enchant armor scrolls.

> Don't try to play wizards as melee fighters.

Agreed, but more. Play wizards as pure pacifists
until at least you've bought enough protection that
you can ditch some of the spellcast-blocking armor
that assists early survival but impedes further
development _as_ a wizard once your naked AC is a
good one.

> Retreat into corridors and use your spells.

Yes, and no, respectively, at least not the attack
spells. I'd _love_ to be always able to cast "sleep"
and "healing", to put nasties into slumber so my
pets can savage them, and to heal my pets, but force
bolt is just a cute way to kick down doors and
remove inconvenient boulders until after Mines Town,
for me, and that's how I exercise attack spells
early on.

All I can say is that if getting to Mines Town at XL
1 were as difficult as some people believe, there
wouldn't even _be_ a "protection racket". Truth is,
it is quite easy to do, after some practice, it just
requires a mindset shift to pure "Rincewind
Tactics": at the first hint of danger, run like
blazes, straight for the protection of your mongo
pet, and don't look back except over your pet's
sheltering shoulder.

xanthian, did I mention having really firm opinions?

Anyway, this isn't a "true or false" issue, this is
purely a "playing style" issue, so we are allowed to
have different opinions and still be at peace.



